#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0439fb8-285d-58ae-8d21-45d07193ac91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}+16({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305233} {:answer/id #uuid "8366b443-8880-5877-9f6f-e1644a1546f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10({h}^{-1}(x))^{4}+3({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}-8({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305234} {:answer/id #uuid "5c817d1d-b88c-52d3-a3f6-c59692c44f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}-2({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305235} {:answer/id #uuid "bd39c4f9-b65d-53c9-9fb5-2875d85a51f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}-3({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-6({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305236} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd2fd1e-80b3-5882-b5ff-8cd62010899c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd2fd1e-80b3-5882-b5ff-8cd62010899c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305237} :answer-field/id #uuid "61bf5b6d-a7af-5039-b409-0dc025a78e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e0661d3-5ec7-5492-854f-de5461f920f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-110045" :question/problem "If $h(x)=5{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}-10,$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=5{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}-10,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=5{y}^{4}+2{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-10\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(5{y}^{4}+2{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-10) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =20{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-16y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{20{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-16y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5e96b4-4200-59ee-bd46-a598d9793898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}-3({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305240} {:answer/id #uuid "24439e3f-7b88-576c-b203-a58131ddfeae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+8({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305241} {:answer/id #uuid "b52ace05-6a2b-5a36-9673-0f75b5d47e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305242} {:answer/id #uuid "a33dc6be-3dfc-5603-8c60-0517320e7874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{4}-8({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305243} {:answer/id #uuid "934008cb-5791-5bf3-bb2c-c0081c588f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{4}-16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b52ace05-6a2b-5a36-9673-0f75b5d47e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305242} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd08776-584c-5ba5-854e-c883f8e400dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca982668-02f8-564e-94b7-165ec7fe8181" :question/math-academy-id "q-110046" :question/problem "If $h(x)=8{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-12,$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=8{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-12,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=8{y}^{6}+4{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}-12\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(8{y}^{6}+4{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}-12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =48{y}^{5}+16{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{48{y}^{5}+16{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{48({h}^{-1}(x))^{5}+16({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+9({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5c52730-54de-5e38-a389-3e120f4c45a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{6{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305247} {:answer/id #uuid "a0184b72-f861-5763-a3cd-ce6af6adea12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{6{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305248} {:answer/id #uuid "7cffd6a6-1759-5f65-a273-3748379d0adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{12{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305249} {:answer/id #uuid "a9210eff-9d8c-5308-b628-21c58fedd8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{12{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305250} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ba0127-5eee-5f95-9504-e984c978dbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{3{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cffd6a6-1759-5f65-a273-3748379d0adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{12{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305249} :answer-field/id #uuid "446e8a1e-c1da-59e2-907e-e7c32653a8fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "372de79c-9c1e-56de-936b-79d5b2d31a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-110048" :question/problem "If $f(x)={sin}^{4}⁡({x}^{3}),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={sin}^{4}⁡({x}^{3}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{4}⁡({y}^{3})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({sin}^{4}⁡({y}^{3})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4{sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3})\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡({y}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{3})}{12{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{12{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{3})}{12{[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4a768f-5a72-55e9-9fb0-6710362e588c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305254} {:answer/id #uuid "1696969b-4a3e-595e-8a84-bd0fb84d8a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c4d1b5-8de7-5b54-bca2-e25be0d8790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{sec}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305256} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2e7d6d-9564-50dc-92ad-175d099572f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305257} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a88da9-95f1-548a-8fb0-6581d617cc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a88da9-95f1-548a-8fb0-6581d617cc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305258} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee805f1-19ee-56d6-bc12-15998edfd539" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10f78469-c4d5-5a89-876b-c2c2c094075b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32639" :question/problem "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6y-1)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "875dde83-6d5d-5126-a003-27e5a8afd84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305261} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf997cb-37b5-5e41-a93f-fafcefe660b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305262} {:answer/id #uuid "79b16cbc-3dac-57fe-9847-0afc8d633fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305263} {:answer/id #uuid "23ed058b-2f3f-506f-ad3d-e40a50a578cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305264} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fcca95-675f-58d5-9b34-23f8c68eb1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7fcca95-675f-58d5-9b34-23f8c68eb1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305265} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa7a972-26ae-578a-bb2a-0acbaa78ab52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "caea86a6-3c6b-5df2-92a6-959f9c7b690c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73064" :question/problem "If $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+9x,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=3{x}^{3}+9x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{3}+9y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{3}+9y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =9{y}^{2}+9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
